+#### Durable project record in Spaces
+
+Use one user-designated coordination Space with a separate top-level project Page per coherent workstream. A short overview Page may link active projects, priorities, blockers, and decisions needing attention. Project Pages do not need to be children of that overview. Keep engineering records on the relevant project Page; do not duplicate units, ledger rows, or the frontier in the overview. The project's own PR and issue overview remains a separate record within its project Page.
+
+Discover the authorized Space and current project Pages before creating anything. Read the relevant Space root guidance, tool-identified instructions, current content, and scoped listings, following pagination. Reuse a canonical record by purpose and content, not title alone. If the intended target remains ambiguous, resolve it before writing. Do not create a replacement Space or reorganize existing Pages as part of initialization. Creation of a missing project Page proceeds only when authorized. Check the lead's actual exposed record tools, successful read access, write permissions, and write receipts before claiming direct record ownership. A tool catalog alone does not prove successful access or persistence. Never expand sharing or copy private sources to a broader audience. Store concise evidence links and sanitized findings, not secrets or raw sensitive logs.
+
+Use the designated writer's active Pages `write-page` and `maintain-space` skills and tool contracts. Read their current edit guidance before writing. For a new top-level Page, the currently exposed `create_page` contract accepts the discovered Space ID with `parent_page_id` omitted. Inspect the invoking host's contract before using it. Never pass a Space ID as a Page ID or invent a folder argument. Verify the returned identity, content, and Space placement through supported metadata and listings. Reuse existing Page identities, content, history, and sharing. A different existing placement does not authorize moving or deleting the Page. If a separate user request authorizes placement changes, follow `organize-space` and any tool-required access preview and confirmation.
+
+For an unknown creation, placement, or edit outcome, reconcile the returned identity and receipts against current content, placement metadata, and scoped listings before another write. Stale metadata or an incomplete listing does not prove failure. Keep unresolved placement explicit and pause dependent dispatch. Never create a replacement Page to repair an uncertain operation. Use the active tool's recovery controls when supplied.
+
+Pages support native Markdown headings, numbered lists, tables, checklists, links and code fences. They are not TSV/JSON filesystem mounts, transactional databases, automatic dependency engines, or edit-trigger subscriptions. Tool names and argument schemas vary by host; use only exposed operations and returned references, never guessed API calls.
+
+Preserve the original pstack record types and fields below on each project Page. These filenames name the source contract, not files to mount or a second store to create. Use supported native Page tables for tabular records, with these columns and field meanings. Keep all fields even when a value is unknown; never replace a register with arbitrary progress prose. Use sections on the project Page, linking longer briefs or research only when needed.
+
+| Original record | Page format and fields |
+| --- | --- |
+| `preferences.md` | Numbered standing orders, one constraint per line. Preserve numbering and current wording for model policy, stack shape and count, verification bar, forbidden paths and escalation policy. Paste them verbatim into briefs and resumes. |
+| `overview.md` | Markdown PR and issue record for this project. Append distinct findings; never rewrite it wholesale for each event. |
+| `units.tsv` | Table columns `id`, `track`, `state`, `branch`, `pr`, `sha`, `brief`. One row per unit, updated in place. `sha` is the exact head SHA; `brief` points to the scoped Markdown brief. |
+| `ledger.tsv` | Table columns `pr`, `sha`, `verdict`, `evidence`, `verifier`, `ts`. One row per verdict keyed by PR plus exact head SHA. Preserve the verdict meanings in Verification. |
+| `decisions.tsv` | Table columns `ts`, `phase`, `decision`, `why`, `evidence`, `result`, per show-me-your-work. Preserve dated decisions and their evidence. |
+| `frontier.json` | Explicit structured snapshot with `generation`, ordered `prs` entries containing `pr`, `branches`, `sha`, `state`, and `lowestUnmerged`. Use labeled scalar fields and an ordered Page table for `prs`, or a supported JSON fence. Preserve stack order and observed forge state. |
+| Briefs and research | Markdown scope, upstream context and source links, acceptance criteria and verify recipe. |
+| `gates.md` | Markdown gates with `id`, status (open or resolved), question, options, default on no answer where permitted, and answer when resolved. Retain the relevant user decision/source. An unanswered approval gate stays closed. |
+| `inbox/` TSV pointers | Table columns `ts`, `agent`, `unit`, `status`, `report`. These are completion pointers, not verification verdicts or chat transcripts. |
+| `status.md` | Derived structured status with `unitStates`, `ledgerVerdicts`, `frontierGeneration`, `openGateIds`, and what changed at the drain. Compute counts from current units and ledger, and frontier/gates from their records. Do not hand-maintain progress narration. |
+
+Unknown values stay explicitly unknown. Do not invent historical generations, exact SHAs, timestamps, verifier identities or evidence to fill a cell. Distinguish an observed empty frontier (`prs` empty and `lowestUnmerged` null) from one that has not been read. A missing prior generation stays unknown until established from evidence; do not reconstruct history from the current PR count. JSONL examples elsewhere describe chat transcripts, never coordinator storage.
+
+Before dispatch, save and read back the current standing orders, scoped units and briefs, known frontier, open gates and derived status on the project Page. If an overview is used, refresh it from those project records when priorities or decisions change. Record a new standing constraint before acting on it. Preserve the small-task collapse in Frame; the Space does not force project machinery onto a task that collapses.
+
+The lead is the designated writer of its workstream record when direct read/write capability is verified. When record tools or access are missing, identify the precise gap and return requested record deltas with source evidence to outer dot. An authorized host with record access can be the designated relay writer. It applies the lead's deltas, checks receipts and readback, and relays confirmed state without taking over technical planning. Never claim the lead wrote a relayed update. If neither host can save, retain the deltas and last confirmed Page link in the current thread, report persistence blocked, and pause dependent dispatch. A local-file substitute is not durable Page persistence. Recovery uses current content and unapplied deltas, without an extra approval gate for an already authorized save.
